@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Admin.Services
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Admin.ViewModels

@*
    The design's audit log.

    The entries here are real and are written by this screen's own actions — the design's
    "all actions below are themselves audit-logged" is true of the admin panes. It is true
    of nothing else in the app, which is the whole point of the notice below the table.

    Deliberately not seeded. An audit trail with invented history is worse than an empty
    one: the empty one tells you nothing was recorded, and the seeded one tells you
    something happened that did not.
*@

<div class="max-w-[1100px] px-6 py-5">
    <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2.5">
        <h5 class="m-0">Audit log</h5>

        @* Beside the title, not under the table. The count is what tells somebody the list
           is a page of something larger, and that is worth knowing before reading it. *@
        <span class="flex-1 text-xs text-ink/55">@Vm.AuditRangeLabel</span>

        @*
            Searched in the database, not over the page on screen. Filtering rows already
            fetched would search fifty entries and report a total of three hundred — the
            reader would see "1—50 of 312" above two rows and have no way to tell which
            number was lying.
        *@
        <input class="input w-[220px]" type="search"
               placeholder="Search the log…"
               @bind="Vm.AuditSearch"
               @bind:event="oninput"
               @bind:after="() => Vm.SearchAuditCommand.ExecuteAsync()" />

        @foreach (var filter in AdminViewModel.AuditFilters)
        {
            <button type="button" class="@ChipCss.Chip(Vm.AuditFilter == filter)"
                    @onclick="() => Vm.SetAuditFilterCommand.ExecuteAsync(filter?.ToString() ?? string.Empty)">
                @AdminViewModel.FilterLabel(filter)
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @if (Vm.Audit.Count == 0)
    {
        <div class="border-2 border-divider bg-surface p-3.5 text-sm">
            <strong>Nothing recorded yet.</strong>
            <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                @if (Vm.IsAuditNarrowed)
                {
                    @* A narrowed log that finds nothing is not an empty log, and the note
                       below about the app barely writing to it would be the wrong answer. *@
                    <text>
                        Nothing matches. Clear the search, or try
                        <strong>Everything</strong>.
                    </text>
                }
                else
                {
                    @*
                        The honest reason, rather than an empty table that looks broken.
                    *@
                    <text>
                        The log is empty because almost nothing in Molargo writes to it
                        yet. Deactivate a staff member, change a registration or take a
                        backup, and the entry appears here — those actions are audited.
                    </text>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full border-collapse">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th class="head-cell">When</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Who</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Action</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Record</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Detail</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var row in Vm.Audit)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td class="body-cell text-xs">
                                @MolargoFormat.DayTime(row.OccurredUtc)
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell">
                                <strong>@(row.ProviderName ?? "unknown")</strong>

                                @if (row.DeviceId is { Length: > 0 } device)
                                {
                                    @* Which installation, which is what an offline-first
                                       practice can actually trace a change to. *@
                                    <div class="font-mono text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                                        @Shorten(device)
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell">
                                <span class="@AdminCss.ActionTag(row.Action)">
                                    @AdminService.ActionLabel(row.Action)
                                </span>
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell text-xs">
                                @row.EntityName

                                @if (row.PatientName is { Length: > 0 } patient)
                                {
                                    <div class="text-ink/55">@patient</div>
                                }
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell text-xs">@(row.Detail ?? "—")</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        @*
            The shared pager, not a second copy of one. This screen's was the original and
            the component was lifted out of it; keeping both would have been two controls
            that looked alike until one of them was changed.
        *@
        @* The gap from the table above, which this screen owns and the pager cannot know. *@
        <div class="mt-3">
            <PagerComponent Page="Vm.AuditPage"
                            PageCount="Vm.AuditPageCount"
                            Label="@Vm.AuditRangeLabel"
                            OnPrevious="() => Vm.PreviousAuditPageCommand.ExecuteAsync()"
                            OnNext="() => Vm.NextAuditPageCommand.ExecuteAsync()"
                            OnGoTo="page => Vm.GoToAuditPageCommand.ExecuteAsync(page)" />
        </div>
    }

    <div class="mt-4 border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
         role="note">
        <strong>What is not recorded.</strong>
        <div class="mt-1 text-xs">
            Draft work is left alone on purpose — an invoice line added and removed, a plan
            edited before it is presented, a note typed before it is signed. The log holds
            the acts, not the keystrokes that led to them. Two other things are missing for
            worse reasons: <strong>reading a list</strong> that shows patient names is not
            a recorded view, only opening the record is; and repeat opens by the same
            person inside fifteen minutes collapse into one entry, so the count is of
            visits, not of times the screen was looked at.
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 mb-0 text-xs text-ink/55">
        @*
            The design's four claims about this log, checked one at a time. Each is a
            specific promise a practice would rely on in an investigation.
        *@
        The design promises every view and change is recorded, immutable, hash-chained and
        retained seven years. What holds: entries are append-only in practice, stamp who
        acted, when, and on which installation, and cover the clinical record, the money,
        medicines, documents, consent, appointments, stock and sterilisation. What does not:
        <strong>nothing is hash-chained</strong> — an entry could be altered in the
        database file with no trace — and there is
        <strong>no retention policy</strong>, so entries simply accumulate — the pager
        above reaches all of them, which is the point, but nothing prunes them — and with
        views and stock movements now logged, they accumulate considerably faster than the
        six entries this screen used to hold.
        Searching a patient works through the detail line, which names them as they were
        called that day — so a patient who has since been renamed is found under the old
        name, not the new one.
    </p>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public AdminViewModel Vm { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// The first block of an installation id.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A full GUID beside every row crowds out the detail column, and the leading block is
    /// enough to tell two tablets apart — which is the question the column answers.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Shorten(string deviceId) =>
        deviceId.Length <= 8 ? deviceId : deviceId[..8];
}
